The Human Lifeline Network:

Reachability-Weighted Bottleneck Optimization on Existing Service Infrastructure

ouadi Maakoul 

AI : chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek, Meta AI


Abstract

The global response to homelessness is fragmented across jurisdictional, organizational, and disciplinary silos. A person experiencing homelessness must navigate a cognitive queue: locate an agency, travel to it, wait, narrate their history, be redirected elsewhere, and repeat. The bottleneck is not the absence of resources but the absence of a coordinating layer that transforms fragmented services into viable trajectories. This paper proposes a mathematical framework for AI-assisted human trajectory recovery that operates on top of existing homeless-services infrastructure rather than replacing it. The central formal contribution is the application of viability theory and reachability-weighted bottleneck optimization to the problem of sequencing interventions for individuals experiencing homelessness or at imminent risk thereof.

We define a finite-horizon probabilistic viability measure V_T(b) over a discretized, factored state space of 15,552 states, replacing the intractable infinite-horizon viability kernel with a computable reachability probability. We derive a bottleneck identification procedure from a causal prerequisite directed acyclic graph, prove that the bottleneck search is tractable with complexity O(|\mathcal{A}| \cdot K \cdot T \cdot |\mathcal{X}|), and characterize the optimal stopping policy for continuous support as a threshold rule on the posterior probability of stall. We specify an architectural design that integrates with — rather than duplicates — Homeless Management Information Systems (HMIS), Coordinated Entry, the Open Referral Human Services Data Specification (HSDS), and existing closed-loop referral networks. We formalize ethical constraints as hard architectural bounds: human authorization of sensitive decisions, user veto as easy as consent, plain-language explainability with contestability, cryptographic enforcement of no risk-score sharing, and structural accountability through disaggregated outcome reporting. We address privacy through federated learning with differential privacy guarantees (\epsilon \leq 1.0), secure aggregation, and identity/case-file separation. We propose four falsifiable empirical tests with thresholds calibrated against documented effect sizes for existing interventions. The system succeeds when the individual no longer needs it.

Keywords: viability theory, reachability optimization, bottleneck identification, homeless services, causal prerequisite graphs, partially observable Markov decision processes, federated learning, differential privacy, algorithmic accountability

