Part III: Technical Specification

3.1 Transition Model Estimation

The transition model P(x_t \mid x_{t-1}, a_{t-1}) is constrained by the causal prerequisite DAG and estimated via hierarchical Bayesian models with weakly informative priors elicited from caseworkers, updated with pilot data via federated learning across Continuums of Care.

For each transition x \to x' under action a, the model is:

P(x' \mid x, a) = \begin{cases} p_{x,a,x'} & \text{if } (x, x') \in \mathcal{T}(a) \\ 0 & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

where \mathcal{T}(a) is the set of transitions enabled by action a under the causal prerequisite graph. Priors on p_{x,a,x'} are elicited from caseworkers via structured elicitation, then updated via federated posterior inference.

3.2 Abstention Criterion

The system abstains from recommending when no intervention meets a minimum effect threshold:

\text{abstain} \iff \max_{a \in \mathcal{A}(b)} \left[ V_T(b \mid \text{do}(a)) - V_T(b) \right] < \delta_{\min}

where \delta_{\min} is calibrated to the minimum clinically significant difference. A bad recommendation is worse than no recommendation.

3.3 Federated Estimation Protocol

The transition model is estimated collaboratively across CoCs using federated learning. Each CoC computes local posterior updates over its HMIS data, perturbs them with DP noise (\epsilon \leq 1.0), and sends them to a secure aggregation server. The server aggregates and returns the global posterior. The protocol runs weekly.

3.4 Evaluation Specification

Test 1: Bottleneck Identification Accuracy. 50 cases, blinded panel of 3 expert caseworkers, majority vote. Require top-1 agreement ≥70% and Spearman rank correlation ≥0.6. Pre-register that false bottleneck rate (bottlenecks judged irrelevant or harmful) must be <5%.

Test 2: Time-to-Stabilization. Stepped-wedge cluster randomized trial across 4–6 sites. Primary outcome: 30 consecutive days in permanent housing. Require 15% reduction with p < 0.05, survival models intention-to-treat. The POP study protocol demonstrates this design's feasibility with 20 buildings randomized to four 6-month intervention waves .

Test 3: Trajectory Durability. 12-month return to homelessness, logistic regression with site fixed effects, 10 percentage point reduction threshold.

Test 4: Dignity, Consent, and Autonomy. Qualitative interviews combined with validated scales for autonomy, trust, and perceived surveillance. No more than 10% of users report feeling surveilled, judged, or coerced. Median autonomy subscale at or above midpoint. Pre-register instrument.

3.5 Empirical Thresholds from Literature

The Critical Time Intervention meta-analysis finds small effect sizes on homelessness (SMD = −0.20, 95% CI: −0.35, −0.04) . The prospectus's choice of 15% reduction in time-to-stabilization is ambitious but defensible: coordination alone will not produce large effects, but sequencing optimization targets a mechanism that CTI does not directly optimize.

For prevention, the Toros, Flaming, and Burns screening tools identify the 8% of low-wage workers who become persistently homeless after job loss and 8% of youth receiving public assistance who become persistently homeless within three years, based on analyzing records of over one million people who experienced homelessness .

