Appendix A: Complete Mathematical Proofs

A.1 Proof of Lemma 1 (Prerequisite Constraint on Action Space)

Statement. The effective action space at belief b is:

\mathcal{A}(b) = \left\{ a \in \mathcal{A} : \text{target}(a) = i \implies \forall j \to i \in \mathcal{E}_{\text{prereq}}, \; \mathbb{E}_{b}[x_j] \geq \theta_j \right\}

where \theta_j is the threshold for dimension j.

Proof. The causal prerequisite DAG \mathcal{G}_{\text{causal}} = (\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{E}_{\text{prereq}}) encodes domain-validated prerequisite relations. By definition, an edge (j, i) \in \mathcal{E}_{\text{prereq}} means that dimension j must reach its threshold \theta_j before any intervention targeting dimension i can effect a transition. Suppose for contradiction that a \in \mathcal{A} with \text{target}(a) = i, and there exists j with (j, i) \in \mathcal{E}_{\text{prereq}} such that \mathbb{E}_b[x_j] < \theta_j. By the definition of prerequisite, the transition function P(x' \mid x, a) is identically zero for all x' that differ from x only in dimension i when x_j < \theta_j. Therefore, a has no effect, and V_T(b \mid \text{do}(a)) = V_T(b). Including a in the action space adds no value and may introduce spurious recommendations. Removing all such actions yields \mathcal{A}(b). ∎

Corollary A.1.1. The size of the effective action space satisfies |\mathcal{A}(b)| \leq |\mathcal{A}|, with equality if and only if all prerequisites are satisfied.

Corollary A.1.2. The action space \mathcal{A}(b) is monotone in the belief: if b' dominates b in the sense that \mathbb{E}_{b'}[x_j] \geq \mathbb{E}_b[x_j] for all j, then \mathcal{A}(b) \subseteq \mathcal{A}(b').

Proof. Immediate from the definition: a larger expectation for each dimension can only satisfy more prerequisite constraints. ∎

A.2 Proof of Proposition 1 (PWLC Property of V_T)

Statement. The finite-horizon value function V_t(b) for the reachability objective is piecewise-linear and convex (PWLC) in the belief b.

Proof. We proceed by backward induction on t.

Base case. At t = T, the value function is the indicator of reaching a stable state:

V_T(b) = \mathbb{1}\{b \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{stable}}\} = \sum_{x \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{stable}}} b(x)

This is linear in b, hence PWLC (with a single \alpha-vector \alpha(x) = \mathbb{1}\{x \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{stable}}\}).

Inductive step. Assume V_{t+1}(b) is PWLC, representable as:

V_{t+1}(b) = \max_{\alpha \in \Gamma_{t+1}} \sum_{x \in \mathcal{X}} \alpha(x) b(x)

The Bellman backup for the reachability objective is:

V_t(b) = \max_{a \in \mathcal{A}(b)} \sum_{x' \in \mathcal{X}} b(x') \left[ \mathbb{1}\{x' \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{stable}}\} + \sum_{y} P(y \mid b, a) V_{t+1}(\tau(b, a, y)) \right]

The belief update \tau(b, a, y) is linear in b (it is a Bayesian update):

\tau(b, a, y)(x') = \frac{P(y \mid x', a) \sum_{x} P(x' \mid x, a) b(x)}{\sum_{x''} P(y \mid x'', a) \sum_{x} P(x'' \mid x, a) b(x)}

The normalization factor is also linear in b. Therefore, V_{t+1}(\tau(b, a, y)) is PWLC in b. The sum over observations y preserves PWLC. The maximization over actions a \in \mathcal{A}(b) of a PWLC function preserves PWLC (the max of convex functions is convex). The addition of the linear term \mathbb{1}\{x' \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{stable}}\} preserves PWLC.

Therefore, V_t(b) is PWLC, representable as a finite set of \alpha-vectors \Gamma_t. ∎

Corollary A.2.1. The number of \alpha-vectors in the exact representation grows as |\Gamma_t| \in O(|\mathcal{A}| \cdot |\Gamma_{t-1}|^{|Z|}), where |Z| is the number of observations. This exponential growth is why exact solution is intractable for T = 180, and why approximate methods are required.

Corollary A.2.2. For the reachability objective (rather than discounted reward), the value function remains PWLC but the \alpha-vectors have a specific interpretation: each \alpha-vector corresponds to a policy that maximizes reachability probability for a particular region of the belief simplex.

A.3 Proof of Theorem 1 (Tractability of Bottleneck Search)

Statement. Under the factored state space and causal prerequisite DAG, the bottleneck search over \mathcal{A}(b) has complexity O(|\mathcal{A}| \cdot K \cdot T \cdot |\mathcal{X}|), where |\mathcal{A}| is the number of interventions, K is the number of particles, T is the horizon, and |\mathcal{X}| = 15{,}552.

Proof. For each candidate intervention a \in \mathcal{A}(b), we compute V_T(b \mid \text{do}(a)) by forward simulation with K particles.

Step 1: Particle filter belief update. Given a particle set \{x^{(k)}\}_{k=1}^K representing the belief b_t, the belief update for each particle requires:

· Sampling the transition: O(1) per particle (given the factored transition model).
· Computing the observation likelihood: O(1) per particle (given the factored observation model).
· Weight normalization: O(K).

Total per step: O(K).

Step 2: Forward simulation over T steps. The simulation runs for T = 180 time steps. At each step, the particle filter updates the belief. Total: O(K \cdot T).

Step 3: Reachability check. At each step, we check whether any particle has reached \mathcal{S}_{\text{stable}}. This is O(K) per step, or O(K \cdot T) total.

Step 4: Repeat for each action. There are |\mathcal{A}(b)| \leq |\mathcal{A}| candidate actions. Total: O(|\mathcal{A}| \cdot K \cdot T).

Step 5: Bottleneck ranking. Computing the difference V_T(b \mid \text{do}(a)) - V_T(b) for each action and ranking requires O(|\mathcal{A}| \log |\mathcal{A}|).

With K = 10^3 particles, |\mathcal{A}| \leq 20, and T = 180:

|\mathcal{A}| \cdot K \cdot T \approx 20 \times 1000 \times 180 = 3.6 \times 10^6

operations. This is feasible on commodity hardware within seconds.

Factored state space consideration. The belief b is over |\mathcal{X}| = 15{,}552 states. However, the particle filter operates on K particles, not on the full belief vector. The full belief vector is only needed for initialization and final ranking, which is O(|\mathcal{X}|) = O(15{,}552) operations. ∎

Remark A.3.1. The complexity is linear in each of |\mathcal{A}|, K, T, and |\mathcal{X}|, and is therefore polynomial in the problem size. This contrasts with exact POMDP solution, which is PSPACE-complete for finite horizons and undecidable for infinite horizons. The reachability objective (rather than discounted reward) admits a more tractable solution via forward simulation.

A.4 Proof of Theorem 2 (Threshold Policy for Continuous Support)

Statement. The optimal stopping policy for the Continuous Support Layer has a threshold structure: intervene if and only if the posterior probability of stall exceeds a threshold that depends on the relative costs.

Proof. We formulate the stopping problem as a partially observable Markov decision process (POMDP) with two actions: continue (no intervention) and intervene (re-assessment, re-recommendation). Let p_t = P(\text{stall} \mid b_t) be the posterior probability of stall at time t.

The value function of the stopping problem satisfies:

J_t(p_t) = \max \left\{ -c_{\text{intervene}} + \mathbb{E}[J_{t+1}(p_{t+1}) \mid p_t, \text{intervene}], \; \mathbb{E}[J_{t+1}(p_{t+1}) \mid p_t, \text{continue}] \right\}

where the expectation is over the belief update.

Monotonicity. The continuation value J_{t+1}(p_{t+1}) is increasing in p_{t+1}. This follows from the fact that a higher probability of stall makes intervention more valuable. The posterior update p_{t+1} is monotone in p_t (likelihood ratio ordering), so the continuation value is monotone in p_t.

Convexity. The value function is convex in p_t. This is a standard result for optimal stopping problems: the value function of a stopping problem is convex in the underlying probability.

Threshold structure. For a convex, monotone value function, the optimal policy is characterized by a threshold p^*:

\pi^*(p_t) = \begin{cases} \text{intervene} & \text{if } p_t \geq p^* \\ \text{continue} & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

where p^* satisfies:

p^* = \frac{c_{\text{intervene}}}{c_{\text{stall}} + c_{\text{intervene}}}

This follows from equating the expected cost of intervention with the expected cost of continuing. ∎

Corollary A.4.1. The threshold p^* is decreasing in c_{\text{stall}} and increasing in c_{\text{intervene}}. If the cost of failing to intervene when a stall is occurring is high, the threshold for intervention is lower.

Corollary A.4.2. The optimal policy is a state-dependent threshold policy: the threshold may depend on the current belief state b_t, not just the scalar probability p_t. In general, the optimal stopping set is \{b_t : V_{T-t}(b_t) < \epsilon\}, where \epsilon is a threshold on the viability measure.

A.5 Proof of Federated Learning Convergence (Adapted from Existing Bounds)

Statement. Under non-IID data distributions with heterogeneity parameter \zeta = \max_{i} \|F_i'(w^*) - F'(w^*)\|, the federated estimation of the transition model achieves:

\mathbb{E}[F(w_G^{(T)}) - F(w^*)] \leq \epsilon + \frac{C\zeta^2 S_{\max}^2}{T}

where C is a constant depending on problem parameters, and \epsilon can be made arbitrarily small by choosing appropriate algorithm parameters.

Proof sketch. The proof follows standard federated learning analysis under non-IID data, adapted for the hierarchical Bayesian transition model estimation.

Assumptions:

1. L-smoothness: \|\nabla F_i(w) - \nabla F_i(w')\| \leq L \|w - w'\|
2. Bounded gradient variance: \mathbb{E}[\|\nabla F_i(w) - \nabla F(w)\|^2] \leq \sigma^2
3. Bounded heterogeneity: \zeta = \max_i \|F_i'(w^*) - F'(w^*)\|
4. Anomaly scores bounded: S_{\min} \leq S_i^{(t)} \leq S_{\max}

Analysis. The convergence rate depends on the degree of non-IID characteristics and the maximum anomaly score influence. The heterogeneity parameter \zeta captures the dissimilarity between local objectives across CoCs. Larger \zeta slows convergence but does not prevent it.

Implication for HLN. The transition model is estimated collaboratively across CoCs with heterogeneous populations. The convergence bound guarantees that the federated estimate approaches the centralized estimate at a rate that depends on the heterogeneity between CoCs. With T communication rounds (weeks), the error decreases as O(1/T). ∎

Remark A.5.1. The bound is conservative. In practice, the hierarchical Bayesian model with informative priors elicited from caseworkers will converge faster because the priors regularize the local updates, effectively reducing \zeta.

Appendix B: Computational Complexity Analysis

B.1 POMDP Complexity

Finding an optimal policy for a finite-horizon POMDP is PSPACE-complete . For an infinite horizon, it is undecidable . This is the fundamental computational barrier that motivates the shift from the infinite-horizon viability kernel to the finite-horizon reachability measure.

The complexity is a function of:

· |\mathcal{S}|: number of states (15,552)
· |\mathcal{A}|: number of actions (≤20)
· |\mathcal{Z}|: number of observations (finite, small)
· T: horizon (180)

Exact solution requires manipulation of the piecewise-linear convex value function, represented by a set of \alpha-vectors . The number of \alpha-vectors needed grows exponentially in the number of observations at each iteration: |\Gamma_t| \in O(|\mathcal{A}| \cdot |\Gamma_{t-1}|^{|Z|}) . Since each new \alpha-vector requires computation time in O(|Z| \cdot |\mathcal{S}|^2), the complexity of iteration t for exact approaches is in O(|\mathcal{A}| \cdot |Z| \cdot |\mathcal{S}|^2 \cdot |\Gamma_{t-1}|^{|Z|}) .

For T = 180, exact solution is infeasible. We therefore adopt approximate dynamic programming with particle-based belief representation.

B.2 Factored State Space Tractability

The factored state space \mathcal{X} = \prod_{i=1}^9 \mathcal{X}_i with |\mathcal{X}| = 15{,}552 is tractable for exact belief updates in a dynamic Bayesian network. The belief update:

b_t(x_t) \propto P(y_t \mid x_t) \sum_{x_{t-1}} P(x_t \mid x_{t-1}) b_{t-1}(x_{t-1})

can be computed efficiently using the factored structure. The transition model P(x_t \mid x_{t-1}) factors across dimensions given the causal prerequisite DAG. The observation model P(y_t \mid x_t) is typically conditionally independent across observations given the state.

B.3 Particle Filter Approximation Error

The particle filter with K particles approximates the belief b_t with error O(1/\sqrt{K}). For K = 10^3, the approximation error is approximately 0.03 in total variation distance. This is sufficient for the bottleneck ranking, which requires distinguishing interventions with effect sizes of at least 0.05 (5 percentage points in viability).

The particle filter is consistent: as K \to \infty, the approximation converges to the true belief. The convergence rate is O(1/\sqrt{K}) for the mean squared error.

B.4 Bottleneck Search Complexity

The bottleneck search over \mathcal{A}(b) has complexity O(|\mathcal{A}| \cdot K \cdot T \cdot |\mathcal{X}|). With |\mathcal{A}| = 20, K = 10^3, T = 180, and |\mathcal{X}| = 15{,}552:

20 \times 1000 \times 180 \times 15552 \approx 5.6 \times 10^{10}

This is feasible on commodity hardware with parallelization across actions and particles. The computation can be further optimized by:

· Pruning the action space via the causal prerequisite graph
· Using adaptive particle counts (fewer particles for low-probability actions)
· Caching transition probabilities

B.5 Optimal Stopping Computation

The optimal stopping policy requires computing the viability measure V_{T-t}(b_t) at each time step. This is the same computation as the bottleneck search but with a single action (continue vs. intervene). The complexity is O(K \cdot T \cdot |\mathcal{X}|) per time step, or O(K \cdot T^2 \cdot |\mathcal{X}|) over the full horizon. With T = 180, this is 10^3 \times 180^2 \times 15552 \approx 5 \times 10^{11} operations. This is feasible with offline computation and online lookup.

Appendix C: Privacy Proofs and Composition Theorems

C.1 Differential Privacy Composition

Each round of federated learning satisfies (\epsilon, \delta)-DP. Applying the standard composition theorems for differential privacy to the sequence of T rounds yields the stated bounds .

Basic composition theorem. The basic composition theorem yields (T\epsilon, T\delta)-DP. For T = 100 rounds and \epsilon = 0.1, the total privacy loss is \epsilon_{\text{total}} \approx 24.09 .

Advanced composition theorem. The advanced composition theorem gives a significantly tighter bound for (T\epsilon, T\delta) when \epsilon_{\text{total}} is large . For the same parameters, the advanced composition bound gives \epsilon_{\text{total}} = 100 . The advanced composition theorem is preferable for long-running federated learning systems .

Practical implication. For HLN, we target \epsilon_{\text{total}} \leq 1.0 over the entire training period. With advanced composition and T = 52 weekly rounds, this requires \epsilon_{\text{per-round}} \approx 0.02. The noise added to each round's model update is calibrated accordingly.

C.2 Privacy Budget Allocation Strategies

Two allocation strategies are considered for the privacy budget :

Uniform allocation. Divide a total budget equally across T rounds: \epsilon_{\text{per-round}} = \epsilon_{\text{total}} / T .

Adaptive allocation. Allocate more budget to early rounds, when gradients tend to have larger magnitude, using exponential decay: \epsilon_t = \epsilon_{\text{total}} \cdot \alpha^{t-1} / \sum_{i=1}^T \alpha^{i-1}, \alpha \in (0,1) .

For HLN, we adopt adaptive allocation with \alpha = 0.95. This allocates more privacy budget to early rounds (when the model is learning the most) and less to later rounds (when the model is fine-tuning).

C.3 Secure Aggregation

Secure aggregation ensures that the server cannot observe any individual agency's model update. The protocol operates as follows:

1. Each agency i encrypts its model update \Delta_i using a shared secret key.
2. The encrypted updates are summed: \sum_i \text{Enc}(\Delta_i).
3. The server decrypts the sum but cannot decrypt individual updates.
4. The decrypted sum is used to update the global model.

The security guarantee is cryptographic: even a malicious server cannot recover individual updates without compromising a threshold of agencies.

C.4 Identity/Case-File Separation

Location-time metadata can break identity separation. The system minimizes collection of such metadata or applies temporal and spatial generalization with k-anonymity ≥ 5.

Temporal generalization. Timestamps are bucketed to the nearest day. This prevents re-identification based on precise timing of service encounters.

Spatial generalization. Locations are generalized to the nearest census tract (population ≥ 1,000). This prevents re-identification based on precise location.

k-anonymity. Any combination of quasi-identifiers (age, gender, race, location, time) must be shared by at least 5 individuals in the dataset. If a combination is unique, the system suppresses the quasi-identifiers or aggregates them.

C.5 Cryptographic Enforcement of C4 (No Risk Score Sharing)

The API has no endpoint for risk score. The reachability layer computes risk scores internally but never exposes them. The only outputs are:

· Ranked interventions
· Confidence intervals
· Explanation strings

Even a system administrator cannot exfiltrate individual-level scores without a threshold of authorized signatures. This is enforced by:

· Access control: The risk score database is encrypted with a key that requires k-of-n signatures to decrypt.
· Audit logging: Every access attempt is logged and monitored for anomalous patterns.
· Data minimization: Risk scores are computed on-the-fly and never persisted.

Appendix D: Transition Model Estimation Details

D.1 Hierarchical Bayesian Model Specification

For each transition x \to x' under action a, the transition probability is:

P(x' \mid x, a) = \begin{cases} p_{x,a,x'} & \text{if } (x, x') \in \mathcal{T}(a) \\ 0 & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

where \mathcal{T}(a) is the set of transitions enabled by action a under the causal prerequisite graph.

The prior on p_{x,a,x'} is a Beta distribution:

p_{x,a,x'} \sim \text{Beta}(\alpha_{x,a,x'}, \beta_{x,a,x'})

The hyperparameters \alpha and \beta are elicited from caseworkers via structured elicitation. For each transition, caseworkers provide:

· Best estimate of the transition probability
· Confidence interval (e.g., 90% credible interval)
· Qualitative justification

The hyperparameters are then fit to match the elicited best estimate and confidence interval.

D.2 Posterior Update via Federated Learning

Each CoC computes local posterior updates over its HMIS data. The local posterior is:

P(p_{x,a,x'} \mid \mathcal{D}_i) \propto P(\mathcal{D}_i \mid p_{x,a,x'}) P(p_{x,a,x'})

where \mathcal{D}_i is the local data. The local update is perturbed with DP noise and sent to the secure aggregation server. The server aggregates the local updates and returns the global posterior:

P(p_{x,a,x'} \mid \mathcal{D}) \propto P(p_{x,a,x'}) \prod_{i=1}^N P(\mathcal{D}_i \mid p_{x,a,x'})

D.3 Cold-Start Strategy

HMIS data are sparse and uneven. The cold-start strategy is:

1. Prior elicitation: Use caseworker priors for all transitions.
2. Synthetic data augmentation: Generate synthetic trajectories from the prior to initialize the particle filter.
3. Transfer learning: Use transition models from similar CoCs (with similar demographics and service landscapes) as informative priors.
4. Incremental learning: Update the model as data accumulate, starting with a high learning rate and decaying over time.

D.4 Model Validation

The transition model is validated via:

· Posterior predictive checks: Compare simulated trajectories to held-out HMIS trajectories.
· Calibration: Check that predicted probabilities match observed frequencies.
· Expert review: Present the learned transition probabilities to caseworkers for qualitative validation.

D.5 HMIS Data Quality Constraints

The transition model estimation is constrained by HMIS data quality. Data completeness thresholds vary by project type :

Project Type Universal Data Elements Program Specific Data Elements – Entry Program Specific Data Elements – Exit
Coordinated Entry 95% N/A N/A
Emergency Shelter 98% 96% 65%
Homeless Prevention 98% 96% 95%
Permanent Supportive Housing 98% 96% 95%
Rapid Re-Housing 98% 96% 95%
Street Outreach 95% 90% 50%

The exit data completeness for Emergency Shelter (65%) and Street Outreach (50%) are particularly problematic for estimating transitions out of homelessness. The federated learning approach mitigates this by pooling data across CoCs, but the underlying incompleteness introduces bias that must be modeled explicitly .

Mitigation strategies:

· Missing data imputation: Use multiple imputation with chained equations (MICE) to estimate missing exit destinations.
· Sensitivity analysis: Test the robustness of the transition model to different imputation strategies.
· Bias correction: Use inverse probability weighting (IPW) to correct for non-random missingness.

